        <activity label="var-wc-timeq" xml:id="var-wc-timeq">
          <statement>
            <p>Many people keep time using a 24 hour clock (11 is 11am and 23 is 11pm, 0 is midnight).
              If it is currently 13 and you set your alarm to go off in 50 hours, it will be 15 (3pm).
              Write a Python program to solve the general version of the above problem. Ask the user
              for the time now (in hours), and then ask for the number of hours to wait for the alarm.
              Your program should output what the time will be on the clock when the alarm goes off.
              <em>Using the <c>int()</c> function and modulus operator <c>%</c> could come in handy!</em></p>
            <p>Store the current time in a variable called <c>current_time</c>, the number of hours to
              wait in a variable called <c>waiting_time</c>, and the resulting alarm time in a variable
              called <c>hours</c>, as shown in the starter code.</p>
          </statement>
          <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
            <code>

current_time =
waiting_time =


hours =

print()
          </code>
          <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):

    def testVariablesExist(self):
        try:
            current_time
        except NameError:
            self.fail("Checking that a variable named 'current_time' was created")
        try:
            waiting_time
        except NameError:
            self.fail("Checking that a variable named 'waiting_time' was created")
        try:
            hours
        except NameError:
            self.fail("Checking that a variable named 'hours' was created")

    def testCalculation(self):
        expected = (int(current_time) + int(waiting_time)) % 24
        self.assertEqual(int(hours), expected,
            "Checking that hours equals (current_time + waiting_time) % 24")

    def testOutput(self):
        expected = (int(current_time) + int(waiting_time)) % 24
        self.assertIn(str(expected), self.getOutput(),
            "Checking that the correct alarm time appears in the printed output")

myTests().main()
          </tests>          
          </program>
          <solution>
            <p>Many people keep time using a 24 hour clock (11 is 11am and 23 is 11pm, 0 is midnight).
                            If it is currently 13 and you set your alarm to go off in 50 hours, it will be 15 (3pm).
                            Write a Python program to solve the general version of the above problem. Ask the user
                            for the time now (in hours), and then ask for the number of hours to wait for the alarm.
                            Your program should output what the time will be on the clock when the alarm goes off.
                            Using the ``int()`` function and modulus operator ``%`` could come in handy!</p>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python" label="var-wc-timeq-sol"><code>

#prompt the user for the current time
current_time_string = input("What is the current time (in hours)?")
#prompt the user for the time to wait
waiting_time_string = input("How many hours do you have to wait?")

#convert the current time and the time to wait to integers
current_time_int = int(current_time_string)
waiting_time_int = int(waiting_time_string)

#combine the two times
hours = current_time_int + waiting_time_int

#use the modulus operator to keep the time within 24 hours
timeofday = hours % 24

#print the time of day that the alarm will go off
print(timeofday)
        </code>
        <tests>
        </tests>
        </program>
        </solution>
        </activity>

